<p>Australia said 13 citizens from families linked to Islamic State had booked travel from Syria to return home. The group was identified as four women and nine children who had spent years in Roj camp in northern Syria. Home Affairs Minister Tony Burke said the government received an alert after the booking and would not help the group return. Australian Federal Police Commissioner Krissy Barrett said police would arrest and charge some individuals after arrival, while others would remain under investigation. Local media said the group was expected to land in Sydney and Melbourne on Thursday, May 7, including flights via Qatar.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Legal limits: Burke said authorities face “very serious limits” in preventing Australian citizens from re-entering the country.</li><li>Evidence collection: Barrett said investigators collected information in Syria when possible despite war-zone conditions and the absence of a functioning government.</li><li>Arrest count undisclosed: Barrett said she would not say how many people were expected to be arrested.</li><li>Child support: Barrett said returning children would be asked to participate in community integration, therapeutic support and countering violent extremism programs.</li></ul><blockquote>They have made a horrific and disgraceful choice - Tony Burke</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Home Affairs Minister Tony Burke: Burke framed the return as a law-enforcement matter, saying any returnee who committed crimes should face the full force of the law without exception. (<a href='https://www.annahar.com/international/world/307088/عودة-13-أستراليا-مرتبطين-بـداعش-من-سوريا'>Annahar</a>)</li><li>Australian Federal Police: Commissioner Krissy Barrett said police would provide updates when appropriate so Australians know agencies are acting and individuals are being held accountable. (<a href='https://theconversation.com/isis-families-to-return-to-australia-with-police-waiting-to-make-arrests-281639'>The Conversation</a>)</li><li>New South Wales government: State Labor MP Yasmin Catley said at least two of the returning Australians would go to New South Wales. (<a href='https://www.perthnow.com.au/news/politics/federal-politics/government-confirms-13-australians-linked-to-isis-have-made-plans-to-return-c-22241300'>PerthNow</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cn0pyny6jk0o'>IS families in Syria have booked tickets home to Australia, minister says</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/05/australia-says-13-citizens-linked-alleged-members-returning-syria'>Australia says 13 citizens linked to alleged IS members returning from Syria</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/05/06/g-s1-120373/australian-women-return-syria'>Police say Australian women with alleged IS ties face charges on return from Syria</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/australasia/article/3352571/australia-warns-arrests-13-people-linked-isis-set-return-syria'>Australia warns of arrests as 13 people linked to Isis set to return from Syria</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/australia-says-13-is-linked-citizens-to-fly-back-from-syria/a-77060174'>Australia says 13 IS-linked citizens to fly back from Syria</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/may/06/women-children-syria-families-australia-return-ntwnfb'>Charges flagged as women and children from IS-linked families set to fly from Syria to Australia</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://arynews.tv/13-australia-citizens-linked-is-members-returning-from-syria'>13 Australia citizens linked to alleged IS members returning from Syria</a> - arynews.tv</li><li><a href='https://www.perthnow.com.au/news/politics/islamic-state-linked-families-to-return-to-australia-c-22241272'>Islamic State-linked cohort to face arrest on return</a> - perthnow.com.au</li><li><a href='https: